  </header>

  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-pill">
      <span>🇳🇵</span> Designed for National Examination Board (NEB) Nepal
    </div>
    <h2>Master <span>Question Tags</span> With Complete Precision</h2>
    <p>A comprehensive interactive study portal covering CDC textbook exercises, board exam edge cases, contractions, negative polarity words, and past model questions.</p>
    
    <div class="quick-search">
      <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M10 2a8 8 0 015.29 13.71l5 5a1 1 0 01-1.42 1.42l-5-5A8 8 0 1110 2zm0 2a6 6 0 100 12 6 6 0 000-12z"/></svg>
      <input type="text" id="ruleSearch" placeholder="Search rules (e.g., 'let\'s', 'had better', 'hardly', 'sheep')..." onkeyup="filterContent()">
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="container">

    <!-- NEB Board Exam Warning Alert -->
    <div class="neb-warning-box">
      <h3>⚠️ NEB Board Examination Writing Protocol (Do Not Lose 1 Mark!)</h3>
      <p>In NEB Class 12 English Board examinations, Question Tag questions are worth <strong>1 full mark</strong> each. Incomplete answers frequently lose marks due to punctuation and case errors:</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="#10b981" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M16.707 5.293a1 1 0 010 1.414l-8 8a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 011.414-1.414L8 12.586l7.293-7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
          Always write the <strong>entire sentence</strong> with a comma <code>,</code> before the tag.
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="#10b981" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M16.707 5.293a1 1 0 010 1.414l-8 8a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 011.414-1.414L8 12.586l7.293-7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
          The tag starts with a <strong>lowercase</strong> letter (e.g., <code>don't they?</code> NOT <code>Don't they?</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="#10b981" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M16.707 5.293a1 1 0 010 1.414l-8 8a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 011.414-1.414L8 12.586l7.293-7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
          Always use <strong>contracted negatives</strong> (e.g., <code>isn't</code>, NOT <code>is not</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="#10b981" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M16.707 5.293a1 1 0 010 1.414l-8 8a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 011.414-1.414L8 12.586l7.293-7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
          A final <strong>question mark (?)</strong> is mandatory.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- The Universal Formula -->
    <div class="rule-formula">
      THE GOLDEN LAW: [Statement] + , + [Opposite Polarity Auxiliary] + [Subject Pronoun] + ?
    </div>

    <!-- Core Rules Section -->
    <h2 class="section-title" id="rules">📌 1. Fundamentals Every Grade 12 Student Must Master</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Basic mechanics that govern 80% of question tag formulations.</p>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Subject to Personal Pronoun</span>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Rule 1</span>
        </div>
        <p>A question tag strictly allows only <strong>personal pronouns</strong> (he, she, it, they, we, you, I) or the dummy pronoun <code>there</code>.</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          Ram and Sita are friends → <span class="highlight-tag">aren’t they?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          The dog barked all night → <span class="highlight-tag">didn’t it?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          Nepal has abundant rivers → <span class="highlight-tag">doesn’t it?</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">The "I am" Exception</span>
          <span class="badge badge-neb">Exam Trap</span>
        </div>
        <p>In standard English, there is no contracted form <em>"amn't I"</em>. We must use <code>aren't I?</code> for positive statements and <code>am I?</code> for negative ones.</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          I am late for class → <span class="highlight-tag">aren't I?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box danger">
          ❌ Never write: <em>amn't I?</em>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box success">
          I am not disturbing you → <span class="highlight-tag">am I?</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Present & Past Simple (No Auxiliary)</span>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Tense</span>
        </div>
        <p>When there is no helping verb present, supply the dummy operators <strong>do / does / did</strong>:</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          Verb 1 (eat, go, study) → <span class="highlight-tag">don't / do</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          Verb 5 (eats, goes, studies) → <span class="highlight-tag">doesn't / does</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          Verb 2 (ate, went, studied) → <span class="highlight-tag">didn't / did</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Possession "Have" vs Auxiliary "Have"</span>
          <span class="badge badge-warning">High Yield</span>
        </div>
        <p>In modern standard English and NEB board criteria:</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          He has completed the task (Auxiliary) → <span class="highlight-tag">hasn't he?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          He has a brand new motorbike (Main Verb/Possession) → <span class="highlight-tag">doesn't he?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          They have lunch at noon (Action Verb) → <span class="highlight-tag">don't they?</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Obligation: "Have to"</span>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Semi-Modal</span>
        </div>
        <p>Phrasal modals like <em>have to, has to, had to</em> express obligation and take <strong>do, does, did</strong> tags.</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          Students have to wear uniforms → <span class="highlight-tag">don't they?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          She had to leave for Butwal → <span class="highlight-tag">didn't she?</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Dummy Subject: "There"</span>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Grammar</span>
        </div>
        <p>When <code>there</code> acts as an introductory subject, it is retained in the question tag instead of changing to <em>it</em> or <em>they</em>.</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          There are 30 students in the class → <span class="highlight-tag">aren't there?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          There wasn't any electricity → <span class="highlight-tag">was there?</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- NEB Advanced Traps & Deep Understanding Section -->
    <h2 class="section-title" id="neb-traps">🔥 2. NEB Board-Level Traps & Advanced Edge Cases</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Specific questions frequently designed by NEB paper-setters to test nuanced understanding.</p>

    <div class="grid">
