          <span class="badge badge-neb">Textbook Classic</span>
        </div>
        <p>Nouns whose singular and plural forms are identical (sheep, deer) or collective plural nouns (police, cattle) take <strong>plural pronouns (they)</strong>.</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          Sheep eat grass (Plural V1: eat) → <span class="highlight-tag">don’t they?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          The police arrested the thief → <span class="highlight-tag">didn’t they?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          Cattle graze in the meadow → <span class="highlight-tag">don't they?</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Trap 4: Imperatives Nuance -->
      <div class="card searchable">
        <div class="card-header">
          <span class="card-title">Imperatives: "Let's" vs "Let us"</span>
          <span class="badge badge-warning">Crucial Difference</span>
        </div>
        <p>Pay strict attention to whether an apostrophe exists:</p>
        <div class="example-box">
          <strong>Let’s</strong> (Proposal / Suggestion) → <span class="highlight-tag">shall we?</span><br>
          <em>Let’s have a break, shall we?</em>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          <strong>Let us</strong> (Seeking permission / Request) → <span class="highlight-tag">will you?</span><br>
          <em>Let us invite them, will you?</em>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
          Negative Imperative (Don't...) → <span class="highlight-tag">will you?</span><br>
          <em>Don't let him swim in that pond, will you?</em>
        </div>
      </div>

